<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangapatna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ranganathaswamy Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>, on an island in the Kaveri River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is a major shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> that gave the town its name. Founded in the Ganga period and enlarged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and the rulers of Vijayanagara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, it stood within the fortress that served as the capital of Mysore under the Wodeyars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangapatna.jpg" alt="Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangapatna" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prakruth Gowda · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Ranganathaswami_Temple,_Srirangaptna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
